WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:10.019
This is a cone-based ray tracing algorithm
for rendering photorealistic images of hair

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:15.320
and furry objects, where the number of hair
strands is typically over a hundred thousand.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.880
Okay, wait.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.020
What do these term mean exactly?

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:25.150
Ray tracing means a bona fide light simulation
program where we follow the path of many millions

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.800
of light rays between the light sources and
our camera.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.379
This usually means that light reflections
and refractions, lens blur and defocus are

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:36.219
taken into consideration.

00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:41.249
This feature is often referred to as depth
of field, or DoF in short as you can see in

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:42.249
the video.

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:47.909
A fully ray traced system like this for hair
and fur leads to absolutely beautiful images

00:00:47.909 --> 00:00:50.100
that you also see throughout this footage.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.170
So what about the cone-based part?

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:58.010
Earlier, we had an episode about Voxel Cone
Tracing, which is an absolutely amazing technique

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.100
to perform ray tracing in real time.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.990
It works by replacing these infinitely thin
light rays with thicker, cone-shaped rays

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:13.510
which reduces the execution time of the algorithm
significantly, at the cost of mostly a minor,

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.840
sometimes even imperceptible degradation in
image quality.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.140
Since the hair strands that we're trying to
hit with the rays are extremely thin, and

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.740
cone tracing makes the rays thicker, extending
this concept to rendering fur without nontrivial

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.770
extensions is going to be a fruitless endeavor.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.759
The paper contains techniques to overcome
this issue, and an efficient data structure

00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:40.740
is proposed to store and find the individual
hair strands, and a way to intersect these

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.750
cones with the fibers.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:47.820
The algorithm is also able to adapt the cone
sizes to the scene we have at hand.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.450
The previous techniques typically took at
least 20 to 30 minutes to render one image,

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.950
and with this efficient solution, we'll be
greeted by a photorealistic image at least

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:03.749
four to six times quicker, in less than 5
minutes, while some examples were completed

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:05.090
in less than a minute.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:11.120
I cannot get tired of seeing these tiny photorealistic
furry animals in Pixar movies and I am super

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.260
happy to see there's likely going to be much,
much more of these.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.950
By the way, if you're subscribed to the channel,
